WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.389 --> 00:00:06.790
As much as we have concerns over GPU and CPU bottlenecks, bandwidth is also a considerable

2
00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:14.310
concern in larger multiplayer games. One vital tool at our disposal is managing relevancy.

3
00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.949
Relevancy is the umbrella term used to describe how we determine what data is sent

4
00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:23.219
to what clients, so what governs whether or not their data is relevant.

5
00:00:23.219 --> 00:00:26.379
For instance if a bullet is fired on the other side of the world, does my local

6
00:00:26.379 --> 00:00:29.780
client need to know about it? I'm too far away for it to hit me, and I'm too far

7
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:34.619
away to hear it, and I'm definitely too far away to see its visual effects. In this regard

8
00:00:34.619 --> 00:00:37.960
we can manage the bullet's relevancy in such a way that we can skip sending the bullet

9
00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:43.140
over the network entirely, avoiding the needless bandwidth consumption.

10
00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.840
If an actor spends too long out of relevancy it will be destroyed, following the same

11
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:51.880
destruction logic we'd experience if we called destroy directly. When it re-enters

12
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:55.320
relevancy, it'll have its begin play called, as if it's been respawned back into

13
00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.899
the game. It's worth noting that relevancy works on an actor level, so we can't

14
00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:05.700
set a single component to have independent relevancy rules.

15
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.260
Relevancy is primarily distance based, so simply put, it's something too far away

16
00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:13.469
from my local client to care. By default, Unreal considers one and a half

17
00:01:13.469 --> 00:01:18.010
kilometres too far to be relevant, but that's entirely configurable inside the

18
00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:22.129
actor class defaults under the net call distance squared parameter.

19
00:01:22.129 --> 00:01:27.000
There's a few instances where we might want to disable relevancy checks entirely,

20
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.000
and set is always relevant to true in the class defaults. Typically we'll use this

21
00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.200
when developing networked services that should always be accessible to every

22
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.760
client, but this is of course down to your particular project specifications.

23
00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.200
An example of an always relevant actor in vanilla Unreal, however, would be the

24
00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:45.799
game state. It's just a replicated actor that every client needs to know

25
00:01:45.799 --> 00:01:50.620
about, regardless of their current circumstances. Similarly, we might want to

26
00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:55.579
enable be always relevant to owner. In this scenario, if we take ownership of

27
00:01:55.579 --> 00:02:00.079
something that's too far away, it'll send its data over the network to us.

28
00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:03.840
In some circumstances, distance is too broad of a metric to define whether or

29
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.439
not something is relevant to the client. We can override the isNetRelevantFor

30
00:02:08.439 --> 00:02:12.439
function in the actor to create a custom implementation. So for instance,

31
00:02:12.439 --> 00:02:15.759
we may want to say that our actor is only relevant to players assigned to

32
00:02:15.759 --> 00:02:20.520
certain teams. If we override the isNetRelevantFor function to only

33
00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:24.560
return true when we have a team ID match, we will only send data to that

34
00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.060
particular player. Whilst distance is a primary means of detecting relevancy

35
00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:33.419
for actors, Unreal will also make an actor irrelevant if the root scene component of an

36
00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:38.860
actor is hidden and its collision is turned off. While relevancy decides

37
00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:42.139
whether or not we should send the data, dormancy controls whether or not we

38
00:02:42.139 --> 00:02:46.419
should even try and send the data. So let's give ourselves a practical

39
00:02:46.419 --> 00:02:50.139
example and see how dormant and awake dormancy settings would actually

40
00:02:50.139 --> 00:02:53.800
play out. So let's say we've got a cube in the viewport and it's always

41
00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:59.479
relevant. Let's also say that the server is changing its color once a second.

42
00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:02.479
Given what we already know, we'd expect there to be hundreds of net updates

43
00:03:02.479 --> 00:03:05.360
constantly checking to see if the value on the client matches what the server

44
00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:10.020
thinks. So in this case, is the server's color the same as the client's color?

45
00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:12.539
When we have a mismatch, the new color is sent over the network,

46
00:03:12.539 --> 00:03:16.969
triggering an on rep, hooking into our color change logic. That's a lot of

47
00:03:16.969 --> 00:03:21.879
work for something we know is only going to change once a second. If we change

48
00:03:21.879 --> 00:03:25.680
our actor's dormancy setting to dormant, we can all but eliminate those

49
00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.319
needless net updates by telling the engine directly, hey, I've changed

50
00:03:29.319 --> 00:03:33.699
something, please propagate it down to the client for me. All we need to do is

51
00:03:33.699 --> 00:03:37.460
call flush net dormancy once we've made a change that we expect to replicate.

52
00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:41.060
For this single update, we'll treat this actor as if it's totally active

53
00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:45.180
and liable to all the usual relevancy checks. Once we've propagated everything

54
00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.740
down, the actor will return to its dormant state and await another flush

55
00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:53.039
to be triggered by the developer. You should really consider dormancy when

56
00:03:53.039 --> 00:03:55.719
implementing network features that only need to replicate their properties

57
00:03:55.719 --> 00:03:59.719
when specifically told to do so. So for instance, say we've got a door that

58
00:03:59.719 --> 00:04:04.500
can be opened or locked. Once it's unlocked, we can set the B is open

59
00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.939
bool to true and call flush net dormancy. So we don't need to keep

60
00:04:07.939 --> 00:04:11.780
checking the state of B is open on the client. As engineers, we know

61
00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:14.379
it's only going to change when we specifically tell it to.
